```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch_dist_2_1
diagnosis_text:
The OSPF adjacency between switch_dist_2_1 and router_core_2 failed because
the two routers advertise the shared transit link (172.16.0.8/31) into
different OSPF areas. router_core_2 places its eth2 (172.16.0.8/31) in Area
0.0.0.1 via "network 172.16.0.8/31 area 1". switch_dist_2_1, however, places
its eth0 (172.16.0.9/31) in Area 0.0.0.0 via "network 172.16.0.8/31 area 0".
OSPF embeds the local Area ID in every Hello packet; when a router receives a
Hello whose Area ID does not match the receiving interface's configured area,
it silently discards the packet. Because neither end ever accepts the other's
Hellos, the Dead timer never resets and no adjacency state machine is
triggered — both interfaces remain in the Waiting state with zero neighbors.
switch_dist_2_1's OSPF process therefore has no adjacencies at all
(show ip ospf interface confirms both br0 and eth0 are in Waiting/0 neighbors)
and can neither receive nor inject routes. Without OSPF-learned routes from
router_core_2, switch_dist_2_1 has no path to the backbone, the server farm,
or pod 1, which explains host_2_1_1_1's total loss of connectivity beyond
its default gateway 10.2.1.1. The root cause is a misconfigured network
statement on switch_dist_2_1 that was changed during the morning window.
Corrective fix: on switch_dist_2_1, remove "network 172.16.0.8/31 area 0"
and replace it with "network 172.16.0.8/31 area 1" to match router_core_2.
The adjacency will re-form within one Hello interval (≤10 s) and full
reachability for pod 2 will be restored.
=== END ===
